course: euphemisms
lesson: 4
title: Soft words for hard things: illness, disability and the body
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: People use euphemisms about the body and illness to be polite, to protect someone's feelings, or because a subject felt embarrassing.
explain: Some soft words are respectful, such as "hard of hearing" or "visually impaired", which describe a person without a rude label.
explain: Doctors and hospitals use calm jargon: you may feel "discomfort" (pain) during "a procedure" (an operation), and a mistake that harms a patient is called "an adverse event".
explain: Pregnancy has many soft and funny names, from the polite "expecting" to the joking "a bun in the oven".
explain: Vivid example: when a nurse says "you may feel some discomfort", get ready, because it will probably hurt.
fact: "A long illness" in a death notice usually means cancer or another slow disease that killed the person.
fact: "Differently abled" means disabled; it is a polite, positive-sounding phrase.
fact: "Physically challenged" means physically disabled; it is mostly American.
fact: "Special needs" means learning or physical disabilities that need extra help, especially at school.
fact: "Hard of hearing" means partly deaf.
fact: "Visually impaired" means partly or fully blind; it is the official term.
fact: "A funny turn" means a sudden faint, fit or small stroke (when blood stops reaching part of the brain for a short time); it is British and everyday.
fact: "Going through a rough patch" can mean being depressed or in a mental health crisis, or simply having a hard time.
fact: "Trouble with her nerves" is an old-fashioned way of saying anxiety or mental illness.
fact: "A procedure" in hospital talk means an operation or surgery.
fact: "Discomfort" in a doctor's mouth means pain.
fact: "Adverse event" is medical jargon for harm or injury caused by medical treatment.
fact: "Women's problems" is an old-fashioned phrase for illness or pain of a woman's body, such as bad period pains.
fact: "Expecting" means pregnant; it is polite.
fact: "In the family way" means pregnant; it is old-fashioned.
fact: "A bun in the oven" means pregnant; it is a joking phrase.
fact: "Eating for two" means pregnant; it is a joking phrase.
fact: "In a delicate condition" means pregnant; it is old-fashioned.
fact: "The change" means the menopause, the time in middle age when a woman stops being able to have babies; it is old-fashioned.
fact: "Has a drinking problem" means is an alcoholic, a person who cannot stop drinking alcohol.
exercise: A death notice says "after a long illness". What does it usually mean? | The person died of cancer or a slow disease | The person was ill for a week and then got better | The person was always healthy | The person died suddenly in a road accident
exercise: What does "differently abled" mean? | Disabled | Very talented | Left-handed | Trained in a new job
exercise: An American sign says "parking for the physically challenged". Who may park there? | People with physical disabilities | People who are good at sports | People with heavy shopping | People driving large cars
exercise: "Our school has a unit for children with special needs." Who is it for? | Children with disabilities who need extra help | Children whose families are very rich | Children who need a free school lunch | Children who are new to the school this year
exercise: "Grandpa is a bit hard of hearing." What does it mean? | He is partly deaf | He is strict with children | He hates loud music | He listens very hard
exercise: "Seats for visually impaired passengers." Who are they for? | People who are partly or fully blind | People who wear dark sunglasses on the train | People who are sleepy | People who are reading
exercise: A British gran says "I had a funny turn in the shop." What happened? | She suddenly felt faint or ill | She laughed a lot | She turned the wrong way | She danced in the shop
exercise: "Dad's going through a rough patch." What does it often mean? | He is having a very hard time and may be depressed | He is walking on a bumpy road full of holes and stones | He is fixing a hole in his jeans | He is growing a beard
exercise: An old novel says "Aunt May had trouble with her nerves." What does it mean? | She suffered from anxiety or mental illness | She had a broken tooth | She was very brave | She was often annoyed with her noisy neighbours
exercise: The doctor says "it's only a small procedure." What is it? | A small operation | A short meeting | A form to fill in | A new rule
exercise: "You may feel some discomfort." What will you probably feel? | Some pain | Some boredom | Some cold air | Some tiredness
exercise: A hospital report lists "two adverse events". What does it mean? | Two patients were harmed by treatment | Two patients were unhappy with the food | Two bad storms hit the hospital | Two meetings were cancelled
exercise: An old film: "She's in bed with women's problems." What does it mean? | She has an illness or pain of a woman's body | She is worried about her friends and their troubles | She argued with her sisters | She has a lot of housework
exercise: "My teacher is expecting." What does it mean? | She is pregnant | She is waiting for a parcel | She is hoping for good marks | She is expecting a visitor
exercise: Great-gran says "Mabel's in the family way." What does it mean? | Mabel is pregnant | Mabel lives with her family | Mabel is blocking the hallway | Mabel is a good mother
exercise: "My sister's got a bun in the oven!" What is the news? | She is pregnant | She is baking a cake | She burnt some bread | She bought a new oven
exercise: "No more for me, I'm eating for two." What does the speaker mean? | She is pregnant | She has a huge appetite | She is sharing her plate | She is eating for her dog too
exercise: In an old story: "Lady Mary is in a delicate condition." What is it? | Lady Mary is pregnant | Lady Mary is very ill | Lady Mary is easily upset | Lady Mary has broken her arm
exercise: "Mum's going through the change, so she gets hot flushes." What is "the change"? | The menopause | A house move | A new job | A new hairstyle
exercise: "Uncle Ted has a drinking problem." What does it mean? | He cannot stop drinking alcohol | He keeps spilling his drinks on the table | He cannot swallow well | He drinks too little water
test: News: "The actor died last week after a long illness." What does the reader probably guess? | He died of cancer or a slow disease | He died in a car crash on the motorway | He died very suddenly | He died of old age only
test: The dentist: "This might cause a little discomfort." Jack says "so it won't hurt?" What is true? | It will probably hurt a little | It definitely will not hurt at all, not one bit | Jack will be bored | Jack will be cold
test: Chatty: "omg Sarah's got a bun in the oven!!! due in June" What happens in June? | Sarah's baby is due to be born | Sarah's cake will be ready | Sarah starts a baking course | Sarah's oven is being fixed
test: A school letter: "Children with special needs will get extra time in tests." Who gets extra time? | Children whose disabilities need extra help | Children who are late to school in the morning | Children who are very clever | Children who ask for it
test: Gran: "Your grandad's a bit hard of hearing, so speak up." What should you do? | Speak louder and clearly | Speak more quietly and gently | Speak to someone else | Speak very fast
test: A hospital letter: "Your procedure is booked for Monday." What will happen on Monday? | You will have an operation | You will pay a bill at the hospital | You will see a film | You will have a holiday
test: Gossip: "He's got a drinking problem, poor sod, can't keep a job." What is wrong with him? | He drinks too much alcohol | He is always thirsty, even at night | He cannot afford drinks | He drinks only water
test: A report: "Adverse events rose by 10% at the clinic." What does that mean? | More patients were harmed by treatment | More patients came to the clinic | More events were held at the clinic | More nurses were hired
test: Zoe: "Mum said Mrs Grant is expecting, so who's she expecting?" What should Zoe be told? | Mrs Grant is going to have a baby | Mrs Grant is waiting for her husband | Mrs Grant is waiting for the post | Mrs Grant is expecting good weather
test: Gran: "I had one of my funny turns at the bus stop." What should a grown-up do? | Worry, and take her to a doctor | Laugh, because she told a joke | Nothing, she only turned around | Buy her a new bus pass
test: A friend says "my dad's going through a rough patch since he lost his job". What does this mean? | Her dad is having a very hard time | Her dad is repairing the road | Her dad is sewing his clothes | Her dad is learning to garden
test: Which phrase for pregnancy is joking rather than polite? | Eating for two | Expecting | In a delicate condition | In the family way
exam: Identify the plain meaning of the expression "visually impaired" as employed in official documents. | Partly or fully blind | Wearing tinted spectacles | Unable to read in a foreign language | Exceedingly fatigued
exam: It is given that a medical practitioner forewarns a patient of "some discomfort". Determine what the patient should anticipate. | A degree of pain | A period of boredom | A sensation of cold | A feeling of hunger
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established meaning of the term "adverse event" in medical records. | Harm or injury caused by medical treatment | An unfavourable change in the weather | A cancelled hospital appointment | A disagreement among the staff
exam: Ascertain the plain meaning of the old-fashioned expression "in the family way". | Pregnant | Residing with one's relatives | Obstructing the household | Exhibiting a family resemblance
